icorecomp @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_PLATFORM@
======================================

A native PC port of ICO (PlayStation 2, retail @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_REGION@), produced by static
recompilation. No game data ships with this build. You supply your own
disc image, dumped from your own copy.

This build runs the @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_REGION@ disc and only that one. It looks for
@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_BOOT_ELF@ on the image, and if that file is not there it says the
image is not an ICO @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_REGION@ disc rather than reporting a bad image.


Getting it running
------------------

1. Put your ICO (@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_REGION@) disc image in this folder, named ico.iso. A raw
   bin/cue dump works too: name it ico.bin. The .cue file is not needed.

2. Run @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_EXE@.

That is the whole install. Nothing is written outside this folder, and
nothing needs to be configured first.@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_LAYOUT_NOTE@

If you would rather keep the image somewhere else, pass it on the command
line instead of step 1:

    @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_EXE@ --disc "path/to/Ico (PAL).iso"

The image is checked against the SHA-1 of the retail boot ELF. A
mismatch is reported and the run stops, rather than failing strangely
later.


What this folder holds
----------------------

    @ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_EXE@
@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_LIBS@
    README.txt@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_UI@

@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_LIBS_NOTE@@ICORECOMP_PACKAGE_UI_NOTE@

Everything else here is written by the port itself, each one when it is
first needed:

    icorecomp.log       the last run's log
    icorecomp.prev.log  the log from the run before that
    settings.json       written the first time a setting is saved
    saves/mc0           the virtual memory card
    saves/achievements.json
                        what the achievement observer has unlocked
    screenshots/        where F12 writes its PNGs


On macOS
--------

This build has never been run on a Mac. Nobody working on the port owns
one, so what follows is what the build is meant to do, not what has been
observed. It is Apple Silicon only and needs macOS 14 or newer.

You need nothing but "ICO Recomp.app" and your own disc image. There is
no Vulkan SDK to install and no Homebrew package to add: MoltenVK, which
is what runs the renderer on Metal, ships inside the app along with
everything else it opens.

The executable and everything beside it live in
ICO Recomp.app/Contents/MacOS, so that is where the disc image goes:
right-click the app, choose Show Package Contents, and put ico.iso in
Contents/MacOS.

The app is signed ad-hoc, which is what lets it run at all on Apple
Silicon, but it is not signed with a Developer ID and not notarised.
Gatekeeper refuses a downloaded ad-hoc-signed bundle on a double click.
Two ways past it: right-click the app and choose Open, which offers to run
it anyway, or clear the download flag from a terminal:

    xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine "ICO Recomp.app"

Clearing the flag is the one worth doing. While the app is quarantined
macOS also runs it from a read-only copy at a path of its own choosing,
so saves, settings.json and the log go to ~/Library/Application
Support/icorecomp instead of Contents/MacOS, and a disc image you put in
Contents/MacOS may not be found there at all. Clearing the flag as above
puts all of it back beside the executable.

If no picture ever appears, the log says which of two things happened.
"no Vulkan loader" means the copy of MoltenVK inside Contents/MacOS was
not reached, which is this package being wrong rather than your machine.
A device name followed by a list of missing features means the Mac is
short of something the renderer needs. Either way the two lines to send
are the ones starting "Renderer:" and "Feature support:", which are also
the last two rows of the menu's Display tab.


Settings
--------

The first time a setting is saved, settings.json appears next to the
executable. It travels with the folder, so a copy on a USB stick keeps
its own settings.

Press F1 (or Guide on a gamepad) at any time to open the in-game menu.
The game keeps running behind it. On a build that opens its own window
(the Windows and macOS packages do) a launcher window comes up at startup
first, with a disc picker plus Start, Menu and Quit buttons; pass
--no-launcher to skip it and boot straight into the game.

The menu has six tabs:

    Achievements  what has been unlocked, and three switches: watch for
                  unlocks, show a notice on one, play a sound on one.
    Display       window mode and size; how the picture fills the window
                  (letterbox, integer or stretch); linear or nearest
                  filtering; whether the output frame is the hardware's
                  visible area ("crt") or the whole window ("window", the
                  default, which is what shows the attract movie's whole
                  picture); widescreen; render scale; an fps readout; the
                  screenshots folder and a Take screenshot button.
    Audio         master volume and mute, then one 0-100 gain each for
                  music and ambience, sound effects, the attract movie's
                  soundtrack and the achievement chime. Every one of them
                  applies to the port's own output only; none of them
                  changes a value the game itself set.
    Input         remap keys, both gamepads and the mouse buttons to any
                  DS2 button, plus stick dead zones and mouse look.
    Gameplay      one toggle, "Run in every stick direction", off by
                  default; off matches retail, where only a near-cardinal
                  tilt runs, and on lets a full tilt run at any angle.
    Debug         log level, the console window, the log file, the
                  profiler period and the frame-rate limit.

The renderer is paraLLEl-GS and there is nothing to pick: the port's own
renderer is not finished and is not offered to a player. For the record,
ICORECOMP_GS_BACKEND still names one for the replay tool and for CI; it
is not something to set for playing.

Widescreen ("widescreen" under "display", off by default) is the one
setting besides the output frame above that reaches into the game. It
multiplies the X scale of the game's own projection block, so the frustum
grows sideways while the camera, the field of view and the vertical
framing stay exactly as the retail game left them. "window" follows the
window's own shape, and keeps following it across a resize; "16_9" holds
16:9 whatever shape the window is. 2D is held at retail proportions.
Which of this game's 2D passes that rule gets right is still being
measured, so if a menu page or a fade looks wrong, turning widescreen off
is the answer, and the log lines starting "widescreen:" are what to send.

Screenshots: F12 writes a PNG into the screenshots folder, named
ico-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.png. What lands in the file is the picture as the
window showed it, without the menu, the launcher, the drawn pointer or
the fps readout, which is why the Display tab's Take screenshot button
gives the same picture the key does. A folder that cannot be created is
logged once and the capture is skipped.

Achievements: sixteen local trophies, the public PS3 "ICO Classics HD"
set, unlocked by watching the game's own progress bits. Nothing leaves
the machine and nothing is uploaded anywhere; the observer reads guest
memory and never writes it, so turning it off changes nothing the game
does. What has been unlocked is kept in saves/achievements.json, one
entry per playthrough. An unlock shows a toast (on by default) and can
play a short chime the port synthesises itself (off by default; its
volume is on the Audio tab). Not every trophy is resolved yet; which ones
and why is in docs/ACHIEVEMENTS.md in the source tree.

Any of the environment variables below always wins over the matching
settings.json value for that run, so an existing script keeps behaving
the same way it always did.

If settings.json fails to parse, the runtime copies it to
settings.json.bad and runs on defaults rather than stopping, so a bad
hand-edit is never silently lost. One bad value keeps that one setting's
default and logs what was wrong with it; the rest of the file still
loads.

Full schema and every settings key: docs/SETTINGS.md in the source tree
(https://github.com/nathanialf/ico-recomp).


Controls
--------

Any SDL3-supported controller (Xbox, DualSense, DualShock 4) is picked up
automatically. A second controller is player 2, which is the pad this
disc's two-player mode reads. On the keyboard:

    arrows          d-pad
    WASD / IJKL     left / right stick
    X C Z Space     cross / circle / square / triangle
    Q E 1 3         L1 / R1 / L2 / R2
    T Y             L3 / R3
    Enter           start
    Backspace       select
    F1              open and close the menu
    F12             screenshot

The mouse is on by default. Moving it aims the camera, which is the right
stick; the left button is square (attack) and the right button is R1
(call). All of that can be remapped on the menu's Input tab, and mouse
look can be turned off there.

On the game's own menus the mouse is a pointer instead. The item under
the cursor becomes the selected one, exactly as the game's own d-pad
navigation would have left it; a left click confirms it (cross), a right
click goes back (triangle), and the wheel steps the selection.


When something goes wrong
-------------------------

Every run writes icorecomp.log in this folder. Before it does, if a log
from an earlier run is already there, that file is kept as
icorecomp.prev.log (replacing any older icorecomp.prev.log), so a crash
log is not lost to the next run's log. icorecomp.log holds the full log
for the run just finished, including the renderer and the Vulkan driver,
and it survives the window closing. If the port fails, that file says why
on one of its last few lines. Send it along with any bug report.

If this folder cannot be written (an install under Program Files, a
read-only share), the log goes to %LOCALAPPDATA%\icorecomp instead, and
then to the temp folder. Whichever it picks, its first line names the
file, and the console says which locations it tried and why each failed.

How much the log holds is the log level, in the Debug tab of the in-game
menu or as "log_level" under "debug" in settings.json. It ships at warn,
which keeps everything the port refused, fell back on, or could not make
sense of, and the failure itself. Turn it up to info for the startup
facts and the frame-time summaries, or to debug for everything, which is
slow. A bug report is more useful at info than at warn.

Double-clicking ico.exe opens no console window. A run started from a
command prompt still prints there. If you want a console for a
double-clicked run, turn on "Show a console window" in the Debug tab; it
applies at the next start. Either way the log file has the same lines.

The log can also carry diagnostic channels, which are never printed to
the console. A channel named here is on whatever the log level is. They
are off by default because they cost frame time.
ICORECOMP_VERBOSE=geom turns on the graphics one: it checks every
primitive sent to the graphics chip and reports, once per field, the ones
projected behind the eye or stretched past the hardware guard band, along
with which VU1 microprogram built them. If the picture is wrong, those
lines say so in numbers.

Requirements: a GPU and driver with Vulkan 1.1 or newer. The renderer
names the device it selected in the log.


Environment variables
---------------------

    ICORECOMP_LOG=path        write the log somewhere else ("-" disables)
    ICORECOMP_LOG_LEVEL=name  error, warn (the default), info or debug
    ICORECOMP_VERBOSE=list    extra diagnostic channels, comma separated,
                              or "all". Off by default: "geom" re-reads
                              every packet sent to the graphics chip, so
                              it costs frame time and is only worth it
                              when diagnosing the picture.
    ICORECOMP_NO_AUDIO=1      open no audio device
    ICORECOMP_GS=dump         headless, no window
    ICORECOMP_MAX_VBLANKS=N   stop after N fields
    ICORECOMP_SAVES_DIR=path  memory card location
    ICORECOMP_WAV_CAPTURE=out.wav
                              record the audio mix


